1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,774
This demo's store page sells fifteen languages.
The build ships six.

2
00:00:05,024 --> 00:00:08,387
And which six is the most interesting
thing in the whole download.

3
00:00:12,237 --> 00:00:15,835
Let's do the game first,
because a lot of you turned up for the game.

4
00:00:16,085 --> 00:00:22,139
Seaside Wash. You run a boatyard on a pier,
and filthy ships pull in one at a time.

5
00:00:22,389 --> 00:00:28,835
You play a small monkey. His arm extends.
That's the whole traversal system — he stands on the dock and reaches.

6
00:00:29,085 --> 00:00:34,642
Salt, rust and barnacles all behave differently,
and each one wants a different tool.

7
00:00:39,892 --> 00:00:45,763
Between hulls there's a whole second game.
You repair your own tools by hammering the glowing spots.

8
00:00:46,013 --> 00:00:55,463
You patch holes. You wash windows. And there's an arm training minigame,
where a monkey punches a bag under a sign that says: strong body, sleepy mind.

9
00:00:55,713 --> 00:01:04,013
Jobs arrive as email. You walk home, sit at a PC,
and pick the next ship off an inbox that lists the owner, the problems, and the tools it recommends.

10
00:01:04,263 --> 00:01:10,970
It went up on Steam two days ago. Nine reviews, all nine positive,
and people are playing it for half an hour a sitting.

11
00:01:11,220 --> 00:01:12,415
So let's open it.

12
00:01:12,665 --> 00:01:17,099
Six hundred and nine megabytes.
A hundred and ninety-one files.

13
00:01:17,349 --> 00:01:24,395
Unity, on the Mono backend, which is the good case for this channel:
every package they used is sitting there with its name on it.

14
00:01:24,645 --> 00:01:30,673
Before the packages, though —
where does six hundred megabytes of a pixel-art cleaning game actually go?

15
00:01:30,923 --> 00:01:37,865
Three files. One asset group, four hundred and fifty-five megabytes,
seventy-five percent of the download.

16
00:01:38,115 --> 00:01:44,691
That's the ships. Every hull is hand-drawn at a size you can scrub pixel by pixel,
and that costs what it costs.

17
00:01:44,941 --> 00:01:48,957
Now the packages.
This is the entire list of things they didn't write.

18
00:01:49,207 --> 00:01:58,004
Six. Cinemachine for the camera, DOTween for movement,
a particle helper, a utility grab-bag, a JSON reader, and Steam.

19
00:01:58,254 --> 00:02:06,267
I opened a haunted-house demo from the same week that had more than forty.
Neither number is wrong. But this is a small, deliberate build.

20
00:02:06,517 --> 00:02:10,298
And they used Unity's Addressables
system for exactly one thing.

21
00:02:10,548 --> 00:02:17,229
Translations. Eight bundles, a hundred and twenty-five kilobytes all together,
and six of them are string tables.

22
00:02:17,479 --> 00:02:24,003
English. Japanese. Korean. Simplified Chinese.
Traditional Chinese. Vietnamese.

23
00:02:24,253 --> 00:02:26,467
The store page lists fifteen.

24
00:02:26,717 --> 00:02:34,599
French, Italian, German, Polish, Portuguese, Russian, Turkish,
Indonesian and Thai are all advertised, and none of them are in this build yet.

25
00:02:34,849 --> 00:02:43,384
I want to be careful here, because I got this backwards in a short yesterday.
I said the build had a language the store didn't. It's the other way round.

26
00:02:43,634 --> 00:02:51,333
And the other way round is the better story.
Because a demo is where you find out who shows up, and translations cost money, so you pick.

27
00:02:51,583 --> 00:02:58,107
They picked English, and then five markets in Asia.
That's not a translation order. That's a bet on an audience.

28
00:02:58,357 --> 00:03:03,784
So did the bet land? Nine reviews is a small sample,
but you can read every one of them.

29
00:03:04,034 --> 00:03:11,629
Three Simplified Chinese, two English, two Korean, one Traditional Chinese.
Eight of the nine are speaking a language this build already covers.

30
00:03:11,879 --> 00:03:18,586
The ninth is Brazilian Portuguese —
a language the store sells and the build can't serve yet. He liked it anyway.

31
00:03:18,836 --> 00:03:27,502
And the reviews are useful, which is rarer than it sounds.
The praise is all about feel: the sound of the chisel, the music, the little monkey.

32
00:03:27,752 --> 00:03:38,377
The complaints are all structural. You can't run. Every single job sends you home to the PC and back.
The tools feel too much alike, and a lot of the scenery does nothing.

33
00:03:38,627 --> 00:03:50,088
One Korean player wrote down that the mouse cursor disappears during tool repair,
and that a brush and a sponge are hard to tell apart. That's a bug report and a legibility note, for free, in week one.

34
00:03:50,338 --> 00:03:53,152
Here's the whole teardown on one card.

35
00:03:53,402 --> 00:04:03,505
Unity on Mono, six hundred and nine megabytes, three quarters of it ships,
six packages bought in, and six translations for a store page that promises fifteen.

36
00:04:03,755 --> 00:04:08,894
Worth a dip. It's free, it's genuinely relaxing,
and the demo is about half an hour of it.

37
00:04:09,144 --> 00:04:19,430
If you're shipping a demo, take the localization idea. Ship the few you can afford,
put them behind Addressables so they're cheap to add to, and let the demo tell you which one to buy next.

38
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:30,305
That's my read from the outside. If you worked on this and I got something wrong,
say so and I'll pin the correction. Next time, another demo, another file list. This is Demo Dip.
